<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">O'Neill dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ua Néill</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The O'Neill dynasty, Ua Néill in Irish, was a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenel-neogain">Cenél nEógain</a> of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a>, named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-glundub">Niall Glúndub</a>, a high king killed in 919. After their Mac Lochlainn rivals declined in the 13th century, they ruled Tír Eógain (Tyrone) in Ulster as kings. They remained the leading Gaelic dynasty of the north until the Nine Years' War and the Flight of the Earls in 1607.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/o-neill-dynasty.jpg" alt="O'Neill dynasty" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cookieman1.1.1 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:O%27Neill_Coat_of_arms.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The family counts two High Kings of Ireland among its forebears, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-glundub">Niall Glúndub</a> and Domnall ua Néill, and some O'Neills hold that their ancestors were kings of Ailech descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niall-of-the-nine-hostages">Niall of the Nine Hostages</a>. The first to take the surname was Niall Glúndub's great-grandson Flaithbertach Ua Néill. The annals do not mention the family between the 1080s and the 1160s. In 1167 King Ruaidrí Ua Conchobhair split the kingdom of Ailech, giving the land north of Slieve Gallion to Niall Mac Lochlainn and the south to Áed Ua Néill; the O'Neills killed the Mac Lochlainn leadership at the Battle of Caimeirge in 1241.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The O'Neills then dominated Tír Eógain, around the modern counties of Tyrone, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Londonderry</a> and Antrim. When the Norman Earldom of Ulster collapsed in 1333 after the Bruce invasion, the Clandeboye branch took much of eastern Ulster. In 1493 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII of England</a> called Henry O'Neill, King of Tyrone, the Chief of the Irish Kings, and the earldom of Tyrone was granted in 1542. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-oneill-earl-of-tyrone">Hugh O'Neill, Earl of Tyrone</a>, surrendered in 1603 after nearly ten years of war with the English and left for the Continent in 1607; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> declared his title forfeit, though his heirs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> kept using it.</p><p>Branches went different ways after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a>. Some converted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-ireland">Church of Ireland</a> and married into the new nobility, and the Barons O'Neill of Shane's Castle trace their descent through Mary O'Neill's marriage to Arthur Chichester. Sir Henry O'Neill of the Fews sided with the crown in the rising of 1642 yet lost his lands to Cromwellian settlers. Others served in the Irish Brigade of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> or settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, St Croix and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The arms of the O'Neills of Ulster, a red left hand on white, are the origin of the Red Hand of Ulster, today shown as a right hand and used as a symbol of Ireland, Ulster and Tyrone. Legends of a man who cut off his hand and threw it ashore to win the land seem to date from the 17th century, long after the device was first used. Several Scottish families, among them the MacSweens and Lamonts, are traced in genealogies to Anradhán, an 11th-century O'Neill dynast.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “O'Neill dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pressure from Viking raiders, the Uí Néill and the Dal Fiatach reduced them, and by the 12th century their lands passed under Anglo-Norman and <b>Ua Néill</b> rule.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>Richard de Burgh, the Red Earl, was the most powerful lord in Ireland around 1300, overlord of the <b>O'Neills</b> and lord of much of Connacht.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-ulster">Earldom of Ulster</a></li><li><blockquote>Power within Cenél nEógain passed from the Mac Lochlainns to the <b>Ua Néill</b>, and the kingdom of Ailech became the O'Neill kingdom of Tír Eógain, Tyrone.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ailech">Kingdom of Ailech</a></li><li><blockquote>They displaced earlier rulers and became vassals of the <b>O'Neills</b> of the Kingdom of Tir Eoghain.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fermanagh">Kingdom of Fermanagh</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 12th century the <b>O'Neills</b> contested the kingship with the Mac Lochlainn line, which they finally overthrew in 1241, ruling from Dungannon with inaugurations at Tullyhogue.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tir-eoghain">Kingdom of Tir Eoghain</a></li></ul></section>
